CertHub.register({"id":"ai-200","vendor":"Microsoft","name":"Microsoft Certified: Azure AI Cloud Developer Associate","short":"AI-200","exam":"AI-200 (replaced AZ-204)","blurb":"For developers who build back-end and AI-powered apps on Azure: containers, Cosmos DB, PostgreSQL and Redis for AI data, messaging and Functions, plus security and monitoring.","status":"check","statusNote":"AZ-204 (Azure Developer Associate) retired on July 31, 2026 and can no longer be booked, so this plan follows its replacement, AI-200: Developing AI Cloud Solutions on Azure. Microsoft Learn could not be opened directly during this check (network block), so the domains and ranges come from search results that quote the official AI-200 study guide (skills updated April 2026): containerized solutions 20–25%, AI data management services 25–30%, connect to and consume Azure services 20–25%, secure/monitor/troubleshoot 20–25%. Weights are the range midpoints (22.5, 27.5, 22.5, 22.5) scaled to 100 and rounded: 24/28/24/24. Confirm the ranges and exam details on the study guide page before you book.","lastVerified":"2026-09-24","notices":[{"from":"2026-09-24","until":"2027-03-31","text":"AZ-204 and the Azure Developer Associate certification retired on July 31, 2026. This plan covers the replacement exam, AI-200 (Azure AI Cloud Developer Associate). If you already hold Azure Developer Associate, it stays valid until its expiry date."},{"from":"2026-09-24","until":"2027-03-31","text":"AI-200 assumes Python for code samples and adds AI data topics (vector search, pgvector, RAG, Redis caching) that AZ-204 did not have. Older AZ-204 books and courses cover only part of it."}],"examInfo":{"questions":"Not published (Microsoft associate exams usually have 40–60)","minutes":100,"pass":"700 on a 1–1000 scale","extra":"Code examples on the exam use Python. Microsoft associate certifications must be renewed every year with a free online assessment."},"examSim":{"questions":50,"minutes":100},"sources":[{"label":"Microsoft Learn: Study guide for Exam AI-200 (skills measured)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/resources/study-guides/ai-200"},{"label":"Microsoft Learn: Azure AI Cloud Developer Associate certification","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/azure-ai-cloud-developer-associate/"},{"label":"Microsoft Learn: Course AI-200T00 Develop AI cloud solutions on Azure","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/courses/ai-200t00"}],"planWeeks":10,"hoursPerWeek":"6–8","domains":[{"id":1,"name":"Develop containerized solutions on Azure","w":24,"topics":["Dockerfiles for Python apps: base images, multi-stage builds, non-root users, .dockerignore","Azure Container Registry: Basic, Standard and Premium tiers; geo-replication and private endpoints on Premium","Build and push images with `az acr build` (quick tasks) and ACR Tasks triggered by commits, base-image updates or schedules","Tags vs digests, stable vs unique tags, and cleaning up old images with `az acr repository` and purge tasks","Pull images without passwords: managed identity with the AcrPull role instead of the admin user","App Service custom containers on Linux: WEBSITES_PORT, continuous deployment and deployment slots","Azure Container Apps: environments, ingress (external vs internal), secrets and managed identity","Container Apps revisions: single vs multiple revision mode, traffic splitting and labels","Container Apps scaling: HTTP rules, KEDA event rules (for example Service Bus queue length), min/max replicas and scale to zero","Container Apps jobs: manual, scheduled and event-driven","AKS basics for developers: Deployment and Service manifests, `kubectl apply`, requests and limits, ConfigMaps and Secrets, attaching ACR"],"notes":["Skills measured: Develop containerized solutions on Azure (20–25%)"],"labs":["Free account: create a Basic ACR, run `az acr build` on a small Flask or FastAPI app, then list tags and digests with `az acr repository show-tags`. Cost: Basic ACR is billed per day (a few cents), so delete the resource group when done.","Deploy the image to Azure Container Apps (the monthly free grant covers light testing) using a user-assigned managed identity with AcrPull; add a second revision and split traffic 80/20. Cost: set min replicas to 0 so it scales to zero, and delete the environment afterwards.","Free and local: run a kind or minikube cluster with Docker Desktop, write a Deployment and Service manifest for the same image and use `kubectl rollout status` and `kubectl describe pod`. Cost warning: an AKS cluster bills for its VMs every hour, so practice locally and create AKS only briefly if at all."]},{"id":2,"name":"Develop AI solutions by using Azure data management services","w":28,"topics":["Cosmos DB for NoSQL with the Python SDK (azure-cosmos): CosmosClient, create/upsert/read/delete items, parameterized queries","Partition key design: high cardinality, even spread, point reads; hierarchical partition keys","Request Units: point reads vs queries, indexing policy include/exclude paths, consistency levels and their RU cost","Cosmos DB vector search: container vector policy (path, data type, dimensions, distance function), vector indexes (flat, quantizedFlat, diskANN) and VectorDistance()","Change feed: change feed processor with a lease container, Azure Functions Cosmos DB trigger, latest-version mode vs all versions and deletes","Azure Database for PostgreSQL flexible server: allow-listing and enabling the vector (pgvector) extension","pgvector: vector(n) columns, distance operators (<-> L2, <=> cosine, <#> inner product), HNSW vs IVFFlat indexes and tuning (m, ef_search, lists, probes)","Retrieval-augmented generation: chunking, embedding, storing, top-k retrieval with metadata filters, adding results to the prompt","Azure Managed Redis: cache-aside pattern, TTL and invalidation, eviction policies","Redis vector indexes and semantic caching of model responses","Choosing the store: Cosmos DB vs PostgreSQL + pgvector vs Redis for a given AI workload"],"notes":["Skills measured: Develop AI solutions by using Azure data management services (25–30%)"],"labs":["Free account: create a Cosmos DB for NoSQL account with the free tier discount (1000 RU/s and 25 GB, one per subscription) or use the Cosmos DB emulator locally. Load 100 items with the Python SDK and compare the RU charge of a point read vs a query (`container.client_connection.last_response_headers['x-ms-request-charge']`).","Local and free: run PostgreSQL with pgvector in Docker (`pgvector/pgvector` image), store sentence embeddings from a small open-source model, create an HNSW index with `vector_cosine_ops` and run a top-5 query with a WHERE filter. On Azure, the free account includes a Burstable B1ms flexible server for 12 months; stop or delete it when idle.","Local and free: run Redis Stack in Docker, implement cache-aside with a 60-second TTL in Python (redis-py), then create a vector index with FT.CREATE and run a KNN query. Cost warning: Azure Managed Redis has no free tier and bills hourly, so do this lab locally."]},{"id":3,"name":"Connect to and consume Azure services","w":24,"topics":["Service Bus queues vs topics and subscriptions; Basic tier has no topics","Receive modes: peek-lock (complete, abandon, dead-letter, defer) vs receive-and-delete; lock duration and lock renewal","Dead-letter queues: max delivery count, TTL expiry, reading the $DeadLetterQueue subqueue","Sessions for ordered processing, duplicate detection, scheduled messages and subscription filters (SQL and correlation)","Event Grid: system and custom topics, event subscriptions, filters (event type, subject begins/ends with, advanced)","Event Grid delivery: retry policy, event time-to-live, dead-lettering to Blob Storage, webhook validation, CloudEvents schema","Picking Service Bus, Event Grid or Event Hubs for a scenario","Azure Functions Python v2 model: function_app.py, decorators, triggers and input/output bindings","Common triggers: HTTP (auth levels), timer (six-field NCRONTAB), Service Bus, Cosmos DB, Blob, Event Grid","Hosting plans: Flex Consumption, Premium and Dedicated; cold starts, scale limits and VNet integration","Configuration and deployment: app settings, local.settings.json, host.json, identity-based connections, `func azure functionapp publish`"],"notes":["Skills measured: Connect to and consume Azure services (20–25%)"],"labs":["Free account: create a Standard Service Bus namespace (billed hourly at a low base rate, so delete it the same day), send 20 messages with azure-servicebus, abandon one until it reaches max delivery count and read it from the dead-letter queue.","Free: install Azure Functions Core Tools and Azurite, build a Python v2 function app with an HTTP trigger and a timer trigger that writes to a queue output binding, and run it locally with `func start` before publishing to a Flex Consumption app (free monthly grant).","Free account: create an Event Grid custom topic (the first 100,000 operations each month are free), subscribe a webhook from a free request-inspector site or your Function, publish a custom event with an event type filter, and watch the validation handshake."]},{"id":4,"name":"Secure, monitor, and troubleshoot Azure solutions","w":24,"topics":["Managed identities: system-assigned vs user-assigned; DefaultAzureCredential in azure-identity","Azure RBAC data-plane roles: Key Vault Secrets User, Service Bus Data Sender/Receiver, AcrPull, Cosmos DB built-in data roles","Key Vault: secrets, keys and certificates, versions, soft delete and purge protection, RBAC vs access policies","Secret rotation and Key Vault events (SecretNearExpiry) through Event Grid","Key Vault references in App Service, Functions and Container Apps settings","Azure App Configuration: key-values, labels, feature flags, Key Vault references, sentinel-key refresh and snapshots","Application Insights with the Azure Monitor OpenTelemetry Distro for Python: connection strings, traces, spans, custom metrics","Distributed tracing across services, sampling and live metrics","KQL: where, project, summarize, bin(), join and render against requests, dependencies, exceptions and traces","Alerts: metric vs log search alerts and action groups","Troubleshooting: App Service log stream, Container Apps console and system logs, Functions invocation logs"],"notes":["Skills measured: Secure, monitor, and troubleshoot Azure solutions (20–25%)"],"labs":["Free account: create a Key Vault (Standard tier, charged per 10,000 operations, so pennies), store a secret, give your Container App or Function's managed identity the Key Vault Secrets User role and read it with SecretClient and DefaultAzureCredential.","Free account: add Application Insights (the first 5 GB of Log Analytics ingestion each month is free) to your Python app with `configure_azure_monitor()`, call a downstream HTTP API and find the end-to-end transaction in the portal. Set a daily cap on the workspace to avoid surprise charges.","Free: in the Log Analytics demo environment or your own workspace, write KQL that counts failed requests per 5 minutes and charts them (`summarize count() by bin(timestamp, 5m) | render timechart`), then create a log search alert with an email action group."]}],"study":{"1":[["Why should a container host pull from ACR with a managed identity instead of the registry admin user?","The admin user is a single shared username and password with full push and pull rights that must be stored and rotated. A managed identity with only AcrPull gives least privilege, needs no stored secret and can be audited per identity."],["What is the difference between a tag and a digest, and when should you deploy by digest?","A tag is a movable label (for example `v1` or `latest`) that can be pointed at a new image. A digest is the SHA-256 hash of the image manifest and never changes. Deploy by digest (or unique tags) when you need an exact, repeatable image."],["Compare single and multiple revision mode in Azure Container Apps.","Single revision mode keeps one active revision and moves all traffic to the new one when it is ready. Multiple revision mode keeps several active revisions so you can split traffic by percentage or labels for blue-green or canary releases."],["How does a Container App scale on Service Bus queue length, and what makes scale to zero possible?","You add a KEDA scale rule of type azure-servicebus with the queue name and a target message count, plus authentication (a secret or managed identity). With min replicas set to 0, the app has no replicas when the queue is empty and scales out as messages arrive."],["When would you choose App Service, Container Apps or AKS for a containerized API?","App Service suits a simple web app or API with built-in slots and little container know-how. Container Apps suits microservices and event-driven workers that need KEDA scaling, revisions and scale to zero without managing Kubernetes. AKS suits teams that need full Kubernetes API control and custom cluster features."]],"2":[["Why is a point read the cheapest Cosmos DB operation, and what does it need?","A point read uses the item id and partition key value to go straight to one item, about 1 RU for a 1 KB item. A query must be parsed and may scan the index or several partitions, so it usually costs more."],["What goes into a Cosmos DB container vector policy, and why exclude the vector path from the regular index?","The vector policy lists each embedding path with its data type, dimensions and distance function (cosine, dot product or Euclidean), and a vector index (flat, quantizedFlat or diskANN) is added in the indexing policy. Excluding the vector path from the normal range index avoids wasting RUs and storage indexing hundreds of numbers per item."],["Compare HNSW and IVFFlat indexes in pgvector.","HNSW builds a graph, gives better speed and recall, can be built on an empty table and is tuned with m, ef_construction and ef_search, but uses more memory and builds slower. IVFFlat clusters vectors into lists, builds faster with less memory, should be created after data is loaded and is tuned with lists and probes."],["Describe the steps of a basic RAG pipeline.","Split documents into chunks, create an embedding for each chunk, and store the text, embedding and metadata. At query time, embed the question, run a top-k similarity search (filtered by metadata such as tenant or category), add the retrieved chunks to the prompt and send it to the model."],["How does the cache-aside pattern work with Redis, and how do you keep data fresh?","The app checks Redis first; on a miss it reads the database, writes the value to Redis with a TTL and returns it. Freshness comes from the TTL and from deleting or updating the cache key when the source data changes."],["When is the Cosmos DB change feed useful in an AI app?","It lets you react to new or changed items, for example to create embeddings for each new document and write them back, or to sync data into a search index. Consume it with the change feed processor (and a lease container) or an Azure Functions Cosmos DB trigger."]],"3":[["Explain peek-lock and what happens to a message that keeps failing.","In peek-lock the receiver locks the message and must complete it. If it abandons it or the lock expires, the delivery count goes up and the message becomes available again. When the delivery count passes the max delivery count (10 by default), Service Bus moves it to the dead-letter queue."],["When do you pick Service Bus, Event Grid or Event Hubs?","Service Bus for business messages that need reliable, ordered or transactional processing (commands, orders). Event Grid for reacting to discrete state-change events with push delivery and filtering. Event Hubs for high-volume streams of telemetry that are read by partitions and offsets."],["What happens when an Event Grid subscriber endpoint is down?","Event Grid retries with exponential backoff until the retry policy's max attempts or event time-to-live runs out (defaults are 30 attempts and 24 hours). If dead-lettering is configured, undelivered events are written to a Blob Storage container; otherwise they are dropped."],["How is a Python v2 Azure Function defined, and where do triggers and bindings go?","Functions live in function_app.py on an `func.FunctionApp()` object. Decorators on each function declare the trigger (for example `@app.service_bus_queue_trigger`) and any input or output bindings, instead of separate function.json files."],["What is an identity-based connection for a Functions trigger?","Instead of a connection string, the app setting uses a prefix with `__fullyQualifiedNamespace` (or `__serviceUri`/`__accountName` for other services). The function app's managed identity is used, so it needs data-plane roles such as Azure Service Bus Data Receiver."]],"4":[["Compare system-assigned and user-assigned managed identities.","A system-assigned identity is created with one resource and deleted with it. A user-assigned identity is its own resource, can be shared by several resources and exists before them, which helps when a role must be granted before deployment (for example AcrPull before a container app pulls its first image)."],["How does DefaultAzureCredential find credentials?","It tries a chain of sources in order, including environment variables, workload identity, managed identity, and developer sign-ins such as Azure CLI, Azure Developer CLI and Azure PowerShell. The same code works on a laptop with `az login` and in Azure with a managed identity."],["Why use App Configuration together with Key Vault?","App Configuration holds settings and feature flags for many apps with labels per environment and dynamic refresh. Secrets stay in Key Vault; App Configuration stores only a Key Vault reference, and the app's own identity must have permission to read the secret from Key Vault."],["What does the Azure Monitor OpenTelemetry Distro give a Python app?","Calling `configure_azure_monitor()` with the Application Insights connection string sets up OpenTelemetry tracing, metrics and logs, auto-instruments common libraries (such as requests and popular web frameworks) and exports to Application Insights, which links calls across services into one end-to-end transaction."],["Write a KQL query for the top 5 slowest operations over the last hour.","`requests | where timestamp > ago(1h) | summarize avg(duration), count() by name | top 5 by avg_duration desc` (use AppRequests and TimeGenerated/DurationMs if you query the Log Analytics workspace tables directly)."]]},"qCount":88});
